Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.958 --> 00:00:02.874
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:09.800
RYAN MCDOWELL: Welcome
to Google "Cloud OnAir,"

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.630
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.650
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.704
My name is Ryan McDowell.

00:00:14.704 --> 00:00:16.370
AHMED ALTAY: And my
name is Ahmed Altay.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:17.690
RYAN MCDOWELL: Today we're
going to be talking about stream

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.550
processing with Cloud Dataflow.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.820
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.060
We have Googlers on the
standby ready to answer them.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.710
Let's get started.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.140
Today we're going to talk
through stream processing,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.380
common patterns with Dataflow,
the state and timer APIs

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.850
and how to take
advantage of them,

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.010
as well as streaming
with the Python SDK.

00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:40.100
Before we dive
straight into patterns,

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.190
it's important to understand
why streaming matters

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.245
and how Apache Beam
and Dataflow help

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.870
you address some of
the common problems

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.200
within stream processing.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.700
So we can have data
that we're processing

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:55.870
and that data can be big.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.920
For instance, we could process
gigabytes to terabytes of data.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.260
And that data could
be really, really big.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.970
For instance, we
may want to process

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:08.195
huge amounts of data per day
and have many days to process.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.940
That data could also
be infinitely big.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.430
So you could be streaming data
and it's constantly coming

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.872
and it never stops.

00:01:16.872 --> 00:01:18.580
This gives you a
problem with processing,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.032
especially when accounting
for unknown delays

00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:21.740
within your data.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.110
So one of the more difficulties
with unbounded data

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.640
is unknown delays.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.432
Assuming that every record
has an embedded timestamp,

00:01:32.432 --> 00:01:34.890
hopefully, we would see the
records show up into our system

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.020
shortly after the event time.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.985
Here's a record which
happened at 8:00 AM

00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:41.880
and showed up in the
system shortly after.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.220
Then we see this yellow
record that was slightly

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.711
delayed by a little bit.

00:01:45.711 --> 00:01:47.460
And then finally, we
see this green record

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.400
on the right-hand side, which
is significantly delayed.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.430
It had an event
timestamp of 8:00 AM,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.820
but didn't show up into
our system until 2:00 PM.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.910
There's many examples
why this may happen.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:03.960
So for example,
imagine a mobile phone.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.890
You maybe get into an airplane,
turn on airplane mode,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.479
be playing your game
the entire time,

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.280
generating these game events.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.780
And then when you
get off the plane,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.060
you turn off airplane mode.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.510
Then all the events at that
time shoot up to the cloud

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.560
and enter your pipeline.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.950
So even though the events
had occurred much earlier,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.699
they actually showed
up into your pipeline

00:02:24.699 --> 00:02:26.457
much later than expected.

00:02:26.457 --> 00:02:29.040
So the mobile world introduces
this delay in a pretty big way.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.130
If you're performing
element wise

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.350
transforms, for
instance, filtering

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.750
for the yellow
messages, that's easy.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.850
It's just a
continuous transform.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.860
In this case, we
don't care about time.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.930
We're not doing any
grouping or aggregation.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.430
In this case, we don't care
that it's even an unbounded data

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.280
set.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.190
Executing transforms
in processing time

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:52.040
will be just fine.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.240
But what happens when we
want to do an aggregation?

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.900
We may want to see the
count of events per user.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.140
For batch that's easy
because the data is bounded.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.420
And essentially, we
read in the input

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.680
until we run out of input,
and then we emit the count.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.120
For streaming,
you can't do that.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.670
You never run out of the
data so you can't say,

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:19.030
we're done processing and
here's the count that I've seen.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.370
Most commonly, what people
want to do is windowing,

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.330
so divide up the data into
some chunks, emit the count

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:28.150
for that particular chunk.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.710
An initial approach
to doing this

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.780
would be using
processing time windows.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:38.770
So for instance, when a
record shows up to our job,

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.150
look at the wall clock.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.150
Do that for a minute,
and then say here

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.850
is the count for that minute.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.080
There's many use cases
where that is fine.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.652
But if you care about the time
stamp within your records,

00:03:49.652 --> 00:03:51.610
you may not get the
grouping you actually want.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.610
What we really may want to
do is group by the event

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.320
time within our data.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.760
Instead of viewing time
as something on the wall,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.580
view it as a data stream,
group by the event timestamps,

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.080
and output them as we see them.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.780
This allows us to
perform calculations

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.110
over that time interval in
which the events occurred.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:15.820
So for example, here,
taking the events

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.160
of when they occurred
in processing time,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.680
and then shuffling them and
regrouping them into event time

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.610
windows, this allows
us to group data

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.940
by event time, which
would be semantically more

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.320
meaningful to our analytics.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.170
So I mentioned windowing.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.730
What is a window?

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.140
All a window is is a function
to divide data into time chunks.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.080
Within the Apache Beam SDK,
which runs on Dataflow,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.795
we provide several
different window types

00:04:44.795 --> 00:04:46.170
which allow you
to slice and dice

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.520
your data in different ways.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.229
These are some of
the more common ones.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:52.770
So you have fixed windows, so
you can think of a fixed window

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.020
as dividing up data into
fixed periods of time.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.470
So let's say 12:00 to
1:00, 1:00 to 2:00.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.580
And then as soon as you
reach the end of that window,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.780
compute your aggregation and
output a count for that window.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.460
You also have sliding
windows and these

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.650
are sort of like fixed
windows that overlap.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.480
So for instance, an element
could end up in many windows.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.200
So say you have an hour window
that slides by every minute.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.950
You may have a window that's
12:01 to 1:01, 12:02 to 1:02,

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.340
so on and so forth.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.910
And this is useful for detecting
anomalies on the incoming data,

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.320
so for instance, determining
how many of a record

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.600
of a given type showed
up in our pipeline

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.800
within the past 10 minutes.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.130
But what if we wanted to do
data-dependent windowing?

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:48.420
So not look at our
data in terms of time,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.760
but look at it in
terms of something

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.620
that is meaningful
within our data,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.730
such as doing different
windows for every user.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.060
This is what we
would call sessions.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.190
Here every key is
a different window.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.290
And sessions are defined
by whenever there's

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.780
a burst of activity,
we're going to capture

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.190
that burst of activity.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.210
And then as soon as there's
a gap in that activity,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.830
we'll basically
close that window

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.260
and then emit it
downstream for processing

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.641
so that we can calculate
statistics over that window.

00:06:19.641 --> 00:06:22.140
This is a common thing that you
may want to do in analytics,

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.180
and it's supported out of the
box within the Apache Beam SDK

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.010
and Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.740
So we talked about
windowing doing

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.156
and we talked
about, semantically,

00:06:32.156 --> 00:06:36.320
how we would divide our data
up in computer calculations.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.540
But when does the
answer come out?

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.040
This is problematic
because you have

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.880
to know when to
admit the answer.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.520
And this is what we
use triggers for.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.670
So by default, what we have is
something called the watermark.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.250
The watermark tries
to know everything

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:55.560
about our data for
a particular window

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.750
and to know when we
are done with that.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.152
So if the watermark says
12 o'clock, it means,

00:07:01.152 --> 00:07:02.610
to the best of our
knowledge, there

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:07.320
is no data earlier
than 12 o'clock.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.250
And then we can calculate
and emit the on-time result.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.950
For external sources, it has
to be somewhat heuristic.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.940
So for instance, Pub/Sub, we can
actually do ahead of the buffer

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.330
to ensure there's no data
left in it before 12 o'clock,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.860
before we actually emit that.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.770
But if there's a
mobile phone on a plane

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.830
and it has data for
11 o'clock in it,

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.800
clearly they don't
have any connectivity.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.660
We cannot know about the
data that's on their phone.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.050
So there has to be some
degree of heuristic to it.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.610
It can't be perfect.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.170
Just to the best
of our knowledge,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:44.010
we can output a final
count and emit the results.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.440
Here is an animated
GIF with the watermark

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.190
shown as a dotted line.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.440
The y-axis is processing time
and the x-axis is event time.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.050
The perfect watermark
will know about everything

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.300
that we haven't seen.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.530
Perfect watermark
actually sees the future,

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.830
so it can capture data that
shows up significantly later

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:03.580
than we anticipated.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.840
For the heuristic watermark, we
can see that it's pretty good.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.260
But this one element, this 9:00
that showed up significantly

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.690
later, we didn't catch
it because we had closed

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.310
the window since
we would thought

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.530
we were done processing
data for that time period.

00:08:19.414 --> 00:08:21.080
So what happens when
you have late data?

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:22.550
How do you handle it?

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:25.110
In the Apache Beam SDK,
there's a way of saying,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:26.919
I want to process late data.

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:28.460
You can add triggering
to your window

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.106
to indicate you want to
process that late data.

00:08:31.106 --> 00:08:32.480
So for instance,
there's a couple

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.730
of different ways
of processing it.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:38.000
I can say, I want to accumulate
the processing, which

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.789
means keep adding to, let's
say, a sum or a count.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:46.280
So if I had, let's say, a count
of 50 before and two elements

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:47.930
show up after the
watermark, then

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.780
they give me back a count of 52.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:53.930
I can also say, [INAUDIBLE]
panes, which means,

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:58.470
give me a new sum since the
last time I emitted a result.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.310
So if I saw 50 on-time
records and two elements

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.230
showed up late
after the watermark,

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.660
then just give me back the two.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:07.760
This is another
animation which shows

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.000
how we do things with
speculative and late triggers.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.040
We can output
tentative aggregations

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.349
before the watermark is past.

00:09:15.349 --> 00:09:17.140
And then even with the
heuristic watermark,

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:20.090
we're able to update it
and update the result

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:21.670
and re-emit it downstream.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.520
So it's sort of saying, sorry.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.130
Here is the actual data that
I had for this time period.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.710
And this allows us
to capture late data

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:36.841
and also emit speculative data
downstream to do our analytics.

00:09:36.841 --> 00:09:38.840
So now that we understand
a little bit about how

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:42.080
stream processing works, let's
talk about common patterns

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:42.840
within Dataflow.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:49.362
We'll start out with
exactly-once processing.

00:09:49.362 --> 00:09:51.070
So imagine that we're
building a pipeline

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.310
to ingest gaming events
from our mobile game.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.990
We'll start building
this pipeline

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:57.280
from the most simple case.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.240
We want to ingest
JSON data via Pub/Sub,

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.640
process it within
Cloud Dataflow,

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:06.179
and then output it to BigQuery.

00:10:06.179 --> 00:10:07.720
We're using Pub/Sub
here because it's

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:11.550
a reliable, globally available
message delivery service.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.780
And we expect our game
to be popular worldwide.

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.060
Now, if we're analyzing
user behavior,

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.100
duplicate data which
enters our pipeline

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.890
may throw off our
analytics, which

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:24.560
makes exactly-once
processing fairly critical.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:26.840
This is also critical
in other industries.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.340
You could think of
retail where you

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.340
may be processing transactions
or finance in which you

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:33.140
may be processing trades.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:35.210
Having duplicate data
enter your pipeline

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.880
and then computing on
that duplicate data

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:43.430
can throw off your analytics
and skew your data.

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.230
So if we're going to
zoom into the Pub/Sub

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.060
a portion of our
architecture, we

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.970
may presume that
it may cause issues

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:52.280
with our system, given
it's at-least once delivery

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:53.510
semantics.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.270
Here the colors indicate
unique keys of records.

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.460
We can see that on
the right-hand side,

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.150
we may receive duplicates
in the subscriber given

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:04.610
the at-least once delivery.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.710
However, combining
Pub/Sub with Dataflow

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:09.320
allows us to achieve
exactly-once processing out

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.450
of the box with little effort.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.340
So how do we do that?

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.260
Well, imagine that we have
these three messages here.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.480
This yellow message
we're zooming in on,

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:25.300
you can see that it contains
attributes and a payload.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.950
Message attributes are
essentially key value pairs

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.170
that you can set on the
header of a message.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.080
So you could think of setting
a key value pair to do things

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:36.410
like routing a message without
having to deserialize it,

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:39.320
or reacting to duplicates
within the pipeline.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.050
The message payload contains
the serialized payload

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.850
of the event.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:46.580
You'll see that we have
a unique identifier here

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:50.460
within the message
payload in event ID.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:52.700
So the first step to
actually processing this data

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.940
in exactly-once fashion, we're
going to promote that event

00:11:55.940 --> 00:12:00.230
ID to the attributes.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.480
This allows us to
instruct Dataflow to react

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:03.940
to it within our pipeline.

00:12:06.977 --> 00:12:09.310
If we take a look at the code
for our example game event

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:12.190
pipeline, it may look
like the following.

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:14.050
We're applying the
Pub/Sub I/O transform

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.910
to read the data
from our subscription

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:19.090
and pass it to our
transformations.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:22.180
Here the transformations
represent ParDo functions.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:25.540
You could think of a ParDo as
a map in MapReduce, essentially

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:28.290
taking in elements, do
some transformation on it,

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:31.024
and then output the
transformed element.

00:12:31.024 --> 00:12:33.190
In this case, when we're
consuming from Pub/Sub I/O,

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.070
we're just reading the
messages from the subscription.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:37.930
So how do we start
reacting to that event ID

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:41.020
that we promoted
to the attributes?

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.780
Well, it's relatively simple.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.850
We just add in the
.withIdAttribute field and pass

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.580
in the key that we had put
in our message attributes.

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:52.520
This allows Dataflow to begin
reacting to these messages

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:55.340
and then, automatically
within the Pub/Sub I/O,

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:57.530
transform, be able
to de-duplicate

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.300
those messages
before sending them

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.420
downstream within our pipeline.

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.280
However, this will
effectively de-duplicate

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.680
messages introduced
by Pub/Sub, so

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.707
messages that are occurring as
duplicates within our system.

00:13:11.707 --> 00:13:13.290
But there's also
another case in which

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:14.414
you may receive duplicates.

00:13:14.414 --> 00:13:18.229
So for instance, a producer
publishes a message once

00:13:18.229 --> 00:13:19.770
and then they publish
it again maybe,

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.920
let's say, several
minutes later.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.020
This will also handle
that case, but only

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.500
within a 10-minute window.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.330
So for instance, if a
publisher published the message

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:32.610
at minute one and then
published the same message

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.520
at minute three, we'll
effectively de-duplicate that.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.650
But we don't keep around the
de-duplication table forever.

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.280
So if they were to publish
across multiple hours

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.330
of timeframes, then we may want
to introduce a unique transform

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:46.602
to our pipeline.

00:13:46.602 --> 00:13:48.810
In this case, we don't
anticipate that being the case

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:52.972
so we can just de-duplicate
using the withId attribute.

00:13:55.759 --> 00:13:57.300
So another common
pattern in Dataflow

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:01.770
is handling schema changes
from the source system.

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.960
For our mobile game
example, imagine

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.500
we have partners which publish
events into our pipeline.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.600
There may be cases
where those partners add

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:12.150
an additional field
to the schema which

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.220
they are publishing.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.740
We don't want it to be
dependent on those partners

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.840
and have to coordinate
releases, so we

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:21.390
want to react to
that dynamically

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:24.910
within our streaming pipeline.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.350
Looking back at our
messages, on the left,

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.740
we see the schema
which we expect.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.920
We have the same schema that
we were processing before.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.170
But on the right-hand
side in the green message,

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:38.140
the partner has actually
added an additional field,

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:40.660
in this case, partnerDeviceIp.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:45.060
Now, this additional field
will cause the insert to fail.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.590
And this will cause us to
either drop the message

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.200
or continually failing
on that message.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.870
Without accounting
for this, the record

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.660
will never make it into
our downstream system.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:59.470
So we'll have to think about
how we handle this case.

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:02.230
Well, there's a couple of things
within BigQuery and Dataflow

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:05.810
that enable us to handle
this with relative ease.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:07.870
So schema changes
within BigQuery

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:09.370
are additive within
the same table,

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.910
so you can add on fields
within that table.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:15.740
And these changes happen
online and require no downtime.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:18.190
So there's no effect
to downstream users

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:20.620
if we were to submit
an API call to add

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.120
an additional
column to our table

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:23.894
based on the incoming data.

00:15:23.894 --> 00:15:25.810
And finally, the BigQuery
sink within Dataflow

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.270
allows for the retrieval
of failed inserts.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:31.510
So this allows us to
submit records to BigQuery,

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:34.600
then get back all the failures,
and then react to them

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.090
and do things like add an
additional column to out output

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:38.590
table.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.650
So zooming in our
pipeline code, we

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:48.150
can easily handle changes by
retrieving the failed inserts,

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:51.560
performing an update to
the schema in real time,

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:54.930
and then rewriting those
out to BigQuery again.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:58.100
So handling changes
in this way enables

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:02.660
us to inspect each
message for schema changes

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:07.051
and then output the additions
to the BigQuery table.

00:16:07.051 --> 00:16:09.050
Now, we could have done
this in a different way.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:10.800
We could have checked
every single message

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.470
to see if its
schema had changed.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.220
But this would have
been quite inefficient

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:16.850
because we would be checking a
bunch of messages that may not

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:17.760
have changed schema.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:19.250
So you can imagine
that maybe I'm

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.220
processing data at
1,000 records per second

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:24.140
or a million records per second.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:26.840
Inspecting all those
messages where they may not

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:28.490
have changed schema
will significantly

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:30.000
slow down the pipeline.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.950
So doing it in this way allows
us to only inspect and mutate

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:37.100
the schema of those
messages which fail insert

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:39.650
and thus react to those
schema changes that

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:43.000
are happening upstream.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:44.830
So looking back at
our pipeline now,

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:48.740
we've simply added
this additional path

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:50.670
to process those
errors, validate

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:54.710
and mutate the schema, and
then output to BigQuery.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.810
And this reduces the
operational overhead

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:58.880
of coordinating changes
with our partners

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:02.280
and we can automatically update
our pipeline as changes occur.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.670
So we've added a lot of
functionality to our pipeline

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.490
thus far.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:13.660
Now we can utilize some of the
windowing techniques mentioned

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:16.839
earlier to perform
our user analysis.

00:17:19.197 --> 00:17:21.780
Here we don't need to make any
adjustments to our architecture

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:25.500
since some of the strategies
that we had mentioned earlier

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:28.860
with windowing happen
within Dataflow,

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:33.090
nothing needed to add
to the architecture.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:36.360
So an example of analysis
that we may want to perform

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.890
is how long a user
spent playing the game,

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:42.180
and possibly what caused the
user to stop playing the game.

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:47.200
Now, if we were to
utilize fixed windows,

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:51.610
we may run into problems
because if we are aggregating

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:56.080
over a fixed period of time,
let's say, from 12:00 to 1

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:00.610
o'clock, we may have multiple
users using the same device

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:03.220
and then grouping
those users together.

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.620
This grouping would
not be accurate

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.450
when performing things
like how long did

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.360
a user spend playing the
game, how many of events

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.564
of a given type did a
particular user incur.

00:18:14.564 --> 00:18:16.480
And so it would give us
incorrect correlations

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:19.600
over our data.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.160
So what we actually
want is sessioning.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:28.260
With session windows, we can
achieve this per user windowing

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:33.150
and do analysis on each
of the individual users.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:35.010
In this example, for
all elements for a key,

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:38.610
in this case, a user denoted by
these three different colors,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:42.390
are included in the window
until a period of inactivity.

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:45.060
Once that period of
inactivity occurs,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:48.885
we then fire off that window
and then produce our--

00:18:48.885 --> 00:18:51.930
or calculate statistics
over that data.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:54.026
In this case, it gives us
exactly the subdivisions

00:18:54.026 --> 00:18:54.650
that we'd need.

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:59.530
So now looking at
our pipeline code,

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:03.170
we window into sessions
with a gap of five minutes.

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.440
So we'll capture
activity until we

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:09.820
don't see any activity for five
minutes for a particular user.

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:14.410
Then we'll key each individual
event by the user ID

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:18.370
so that we can do proper
groupings on our data.

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:21.010
And then we'll group that
data together and then perform

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.620
analysis on those
groupings of data.

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:27.100
So this enables us to
do things like answer

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:31.570
that question of how long
did a user play the game.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.030
It also allows us to do
interesting things take

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:37.760
advantage of BigQuery's
nested and repeated fields,

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:40.600
for instance, grouping together
all records for a session

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:44.050
into a repeated field and
then having an aggregate data

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:46.594
record sent downstream.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.570
So looking at our
updated pipeline,

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:54.690
we've now added this additional
path with form analysis.

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:58.320
You can see now that we're
sessioning by the user,

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.221
we're keying by that user before
we group that data together,

00:20:01.221 --> 00:20:02.970
and then, finally, we
analyze those events

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:04.655
before we send them downstream.

00:20:07.044 --> 00:20:07.710
AHMED ALTAY: OK.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.680
So let's talk a little bit about
this state API and the timer

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.520
API in Beam.

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:17.820
Beam lets you process
unbounded large amounts of data

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:20.700
in a globally distributed
scale using the Beam's

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.020
portable [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:25.020
And stateful processing
along with timers

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:28.440
adds a new layer on top of it,
which enables new use cases

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.433
and opens up new efficiencies.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.300
At its core, stateful
and timely processing

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:39.600
happens by augmenting
per element operations.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.570
Per element operations
are just things

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:46.830
that we talk such as ParDos
by adding it mutable state

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.680
and also adding a
timer API access.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:56.670
Stateful and timely computations
are the underlying patterns

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:01.080
that Beam builds up
on in other places.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.380
Precisely because it is
the lower level APIs,

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.620
it allows you to
micromanage your pipelines

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.480
and your computations
to enable new use cases.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.510
However, at the same
time, it's not magic.

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:16.440
By managing state
and timers, there

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:17.970
will be additional
complexes that's

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:20.240
built into your pipelines.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:24.840
State API works by adding
scalability and consistency

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:29.560
on top of a fine-grained
stateful processing.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.740
This is built on top
of Beam's portability.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:34.750
It works by allowing
setting per key state

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:38.280
that's accessible within
per element transforms.

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:42.840
With this API, ParDos and any
other per element transforms

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:46.680
can access a mutable state
that is backed by a data store,

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.290
and can read and mutate these
data in a concurrent and safe

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:51.450
way.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:55.500
And similarly, timer API
allows the stateful [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.890
to set timers, either in
the processing time domain

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:03.090
or in the event time
domain, to get callbacks

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:07.380
based on some timer event that's
customizable by the users.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:09.227
And timer callbacks
also have access

00:22:09.227 --> 00:22:10.560
to the same exact stateful APIs.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:18.690
So let's look at the details
of the implementation.

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:22.520
So first thing is status
partitioned by key and window.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:26.000
And what do we mean by
that is the stateful ParDo

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:29.630
in the middle of that pipeline
has access to a data store.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.610
And given an element,
the runner will

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:33.890
autodetect the window
and the key that's

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:35.210
associated with that element.

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:38.750
And that stateful ParDo will
be able to access the state

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:41.920
cell within this data store.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:45.740
And why is it important
that state is partitioned

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:50.180
along the windowing domain
in addition to the key domain

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:53.220
is this allows
runners to clean up.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.630
Once windows expires,
it's possible for runners

00:22:56.630 --> 00:22:59.475
to go and clean up
the state store.

00:22:59.475 --> 00:23:01.910
This is really important
because otherwise, the runners

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:04.140
will have [INAUDIBLE]
state forever

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:05.881
during the runtime
of department.

00:23:08.287 --> 00:23:09.745
There are additional
considerations

00:23:09.745 --> 00:23:12.385
that comes with partitioning
this state per key

00:23:12.385 --> 00:23:13.700
and per window.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:17.980
One consideration is since
there are concurrency guarantees

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:20.110
and the partitioning
happens per key

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:24.250
and per window, that could mean
that a runner need to shuffle

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:27.565
data to a single worker
in order to execute

00:23:27.565 --> 00:23:30.240
within the context of a
per key and per element.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.230
So there are two
implications to that.

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:35.315
The first implication is
there could be shuffling.

00:23:35.315 --> 00:23:37.630
The second implication
is that there

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:39.790
is a limit to parallelization.

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:43.870
It's possible that within
the same key and same window,

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:47.491
the runner will be forced
to execute serially.

00:23:47.491 --> 00:23:50.380
And the other
implication of this

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:54.080
comes in the storage and
fault tolerance area.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.355
Since we are partitioning
per key and per window,

00:23:57.355 --> 00:23:59.890
that means that the state
store needs to keep state

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:01.060
per key and per window.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.910
If there will be a lot
of keys and windows,

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:06.130
that means that there will
be a proportional increase

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.332
in this storage used.

00:24:08.332 --> 00:24:10.240
And the correllator
of that is if you

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:12.400
want to get a lot of
parallelism into your system,

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:15.502
you could partition a lot within
the key or within the window

00:24:15.502 --> 00:24:16.960
or in any combination
of those two.

00:24:20.056 --> 00:24:24.860
So let's look at an example
to clarify a little bit.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:28.110
Here we are talking about,
you can get a regular DoFn.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:32.540
It takes a key value input
and returns some other thing.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:34.280
And the first thing
that you will notice

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.440
is there is a tag,
your state ID,

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.950
which uses an identify index.

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:42.080
Here index is a
mutable state cell

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:44.866
that is partitioned
by key and window.

00:24:44.866 --> 00:24:48.320
And if you go down a little bit
and look at the process element

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.390
function, in the
argument list, you

00:24:50.390 --> 00:24:53.660
will see the same state ID
tag with a matching index

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.130
identifier.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.260
At execution time,
runner will pass

00:24:57.260 --> 00:25:00.860
us the state cell
that we can access

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:03.770
and because of the
matching identifiers,

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:07.520
it will be the exact
same thing as we defined.

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:11.150
If you look at the body
of the process function,

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:12.560
it is a treeline function.

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:14.600
First thing you should
notice is the second line

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.480
is just a regular DoFn syntax.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:21.380
It is outputting an element
based on its inputs.

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:24.430
The first line within
the [INAUDIBLE] body,

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.320
it's reading an integer that
is stored in the state cell.

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:35.330
And the third line is
updating this value

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:37.310
by writing it on top of it.

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:41.170
Since Beam runners provides
the concurrency guarantees,

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:45.740
writing into the same state
will be guaranteed to not try it

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:48.680
on top of undefined values.

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:50.720
Another thing I would
like to talk about here

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:52.940
is the type of state.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:55.100
If you notice, the type
of state, the index,

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:56.830
is an integer here.

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.180
And since I said
that the runners may

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:01.670
need to shuffle
data across workers,

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:04.460
the state value needs
to be serializable.

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:07.040
In this case, the integer
value is serializable

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:10.190
and we are using a variant
caller to ensure that.

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:12.560
So the important
part to get here

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:15.400
is whatever you put in
your state, it's up to you.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.840
However, it needs to
have a caller that

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:20.888
could serialize the type
of your state information.

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:26.240
Let's tie this a little
bit with the timer API.

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:28.850
What does timer API do?

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.640
Any stateful DoFn
could set timers,

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:35.090
and timers could be triggered
in the processing time

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:36.690
or in the event time.

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:38.360
So what do we mean by that?

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.570
Timers could be triggered
within the processing time.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:44.540
That means that a
stateful DoFn can

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:50.480
set a timer that will trigger,
let's say, a minute from now.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:54.830
Or a stateful DoFn can set
a timer in the event time,

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:59.570
and what that could mean is
it DoFn could request a timer

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:03.830
to trigger random timestamps
of elements being processed

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:07.580
reaches a certain timestamp.

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:10.670
Timing events has access to
the same state information

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:13.490
and have access to the
same mutable state data.

00:27:13.490 --> 00:27:16.858
And in combination, they are
not really powerful scenarios.

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:24.340
So this is a combined example
we are looking at right now.

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:27.880
And again, this is a
regular DoFn function.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:29.550
It's this stateful
DoFn function.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:34.110
You can notice the state ID
tags on a bunch of variables.

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:37.140
And one thing that's different
here is the timer ID tag.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:40.530
We are using timer ID tag
to create a new timer.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:45.490
In this case our timer has
an identifier called expiry.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:47.490
And the other thing
to notice here

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:51.690
is timer was created in
the event time domain.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:53.970
So that means that we
can't use this timer

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:57.210
to set triggers that will
trigger in the event time

00:27:57.210 --> 00:27:59.140
domain.

00:27:59.140 --> 00:28:01.920
If you look at our process
method, in the argument list,

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.720
there would be one
[INAUDIBLE] argument

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.930
that is the timer ID tag with
the same exact identifier

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:08.040
expiry.

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:10.620
Our Beam runner will
pass us this timer

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:12.300
and it will do the
matching [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.510
based on the matching
identifier of expiry.

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:18.180
Within the body
of the DoFn, it is

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:22.860
possible to do any of
your regular DoFn logic.

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:25.620
After that, before that,
or in between that,

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:28.770
you can access state
APIs and timer APIs.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:31.080
In this case, the
DoFn is setting

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:36.150
a timer that's going to trigger
after the end of the window

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:39.410
plus some allowed lateness.

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:41.430
And one thing I would
like to point out here

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:44.610
is we are setting this timer
within the process element

00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:47.850
function, and this is
happening for every element.

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.340
This is fine because
setting and resetting

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:55.840
timers in Beam timer APIs is
not an expensive operation.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:29:00.030
Let's go down a little bit to
look at our callback method.

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:03.360
Their method called onExpiry
will be our callback method.

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:06.810
And Beam runners identify
this and understand this

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:09.210
by the tag onTimer.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:14.340
OnTimer tags tags our callback
method used by the action

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:17.160
of using the same
identifier-- that's expiry--

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:21.450
Beam runner can match this
callback event to the timer

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:24.050
that we were already using.

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:28.560
And within the argument list
of this callback method,

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:30.510
you will notice that
it actually has access

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:34.620
to one of the state
in the state cells.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.680
So this is because timer
callbacks have regular access

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:39.800
to the state APIs.

00:29:39.800 --> 00:29:43.750
And to summarize what's
happening within this callback

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:47.790
method, it is reading some input
information from the state,

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:51.100
adding some management,
and outputting as usual.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:55.990
So in combination, this example
uses state API, timer API,

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:57.930
demonstrate callbacks,
and demonstrate

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:00.400
use of state and timer tags.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:10.660
I would also like to talk a
little bit about the Python SDK

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:14.038
within Beam, within the context
of the Dataflow streaming.

00:30:16.850 --> 00:30:20.000
Python streaming is one
of their latest offerings.

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:24.770
Python streaming beta was
launched in July 2018.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.350
From alpha to beta we made
significant improvements

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:30.230
in performance and
stability, and in terms

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:33.440
of the amount of Beam
model of that is supported.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:37.820
In its current state, Python
streaming SDK for Apache Beam

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:40.690
supports all of Beam model.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:43.400
It allows reading
from Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:45.260
It allows writing
to Cloud Pub/Sub

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:48.850
and it allows writing
to Cloud BigQuery.

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:51.320
And what is on our roadmap?

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:53.420
One thing that's
on our roadmap is

00:30:53.420 --> 00:30:55.760
continuing to improve
performance and stability

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:57.980
of Python streaming.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:01.190
The next thing is
we are planning

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:03.140
to add more and more I/Os.

00:31:03.140 --> 00:31:07.810
That is adding support for
other Google Cloud data sources

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:09.800
and as well as other
open source data

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:13.257
sources such as Apache Kafka.

00:31:13.257 --> 00:31:14.840
Another feature that
is on the roadmap

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:17.030
is called cross
language transforms.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.330
This is one of the
most exciting features.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.410
Cross language transforms
allow accessing

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:26.360
to transforms or I/Os that are
written in other languages.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.840
With cross language
transforms, it

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.260
will be possible to
use Python SDK to use

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:33.980
any of the areas that
were already developed

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:36.920
and has been used for a
while within the Java SDK.

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:39.710
So this will open
up Python SDK users

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:44.030
to a large new set of I/Os.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:45.940
The next thing we
have on our roadmap

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:49.820
is adding support for all
the Cloud Dataflow service

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:50.720
features.

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.630
These are the
coolest features that

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:56.850
make people use managed Cloud
Dataflow servers to execute

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:58.370
their Beam pipelines.

00:31:58.370 --> 00:32:00.410
So one of those
features is autoscaling.

00:32:00.410 --> 00:32:04.510
Autoscaling allows picking just
the right amount of workers

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.802
automatically for
Cloud Dataflow jobs.

00:32:07.802 --> 00:32:09.260
So this will be
one of the features

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.440
that we are planning to
add for Python streaming.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:15.170
And finally, Python 3 support.

00:32:15.170 --> 00:32:18.110
This is a longtime
requested feature.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:20.750
We are building this
into the Python streaming

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:23.180
and also for Python batch.

00:32:23.180 --> 00:32:25.421
And this is one of the--

00:32:25.421 --> 00:32:27.920
this concludes one of the major
items that's on our roadmap.

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:32.780
And I would also like to
talk about runners, where you

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:34.960
can run Python streaming today.

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.870
You can run Python streaming
SDK on Cloud Dataflow.

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:41.450
This is supported
at the beta level.

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:44.620
You can use the DirectRunner
for local execution.

00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:46.980
DirectRunner is a
really cool feature

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:48.800
that's a pretty
complete runner that

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:52.520
can execute Python SDK pipelines
within a single computer.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:56.600
It's great for testing and
developing new pipelines.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:33:01.520
And another thing is that
there is work going on

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:04.640
to enable Python
streaming SDK to work

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:06.800
on other open-source runners.

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:09.030
Apache Flink will
be one of them.

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:13.640
So this will just expand on
the synergy of Apache Beam

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:16.400
by combining other open-source
projects together and tying

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:17.000
them together.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.860
So let's look at how a
Python streaming WordCount

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:29.500
example works.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.890
The first thing to notice
within this example

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:34.800
is it is the usual
batch WordCount example

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:37.170
with just a few differences.

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:41.510
The very first difference
is the first line.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:45.390
That is a read that's an I/O
operation to read from Pub/Sub.

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:48.590
Cloud Pub/Sub is
streaming source

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:51.020
and we are subscribing
to a subscription

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:54.570
here to read incoming
messages as they come along.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:56.530
So this will be a streaming job.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:58.400
And as long as the
job is running,

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:01.100
it will be reading new
messages from Cloud Pub/Sub

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.080
as they arrive.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:05.420
The other difference that
I would like to point out

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:07.730
is the second to last line.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:11.090
That is a write operation
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:15.710
Again, Cloud Pub/Sub
as a streaming pipe.

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:19.219
And reading and writing into
that requires a streaming job.

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:24.090
In this case, once the
inputs are processed,

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:25.969
we are writing them
back into the Cloud

00:34:25.969 --> 00:34:28.670
Pub/Sub in a streaming fashion.

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:31.260
Beyond that, there are
really no other differences.

00:34:31.260 --> 00:34:34.340
It is read from an
I/O that is streaming,

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:36.860
do the usual
processing, write back

00:34:36.860 --> 00:34:39.420
to an I/O that
supports streaming.

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:41.810
And just a quick
highlight for the people

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.909
who are not very familiar
with the Python SDK here

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:48.500
is [INAUDIBLE] is the transform
that we use for windowing.

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:50.420
That is the same
thing that we started

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:53.522
talking about at the beginning
of this presentation.

00:34:53.522 --> 00:34:54.980
The other interesting
thing here is

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:58.040
group by key, which respects
the windowing as it's

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:00.410
defined in the previous
[INAUDIBLE] transform,

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:03.830
and groups element
into the categories

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:06.680
that's based on the
key and the window.

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:08.480
And finally, all
the other transforms

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:09.590
that are mentioned here--

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:14.660
Map, ParDo-- are
element-based transforms.

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:16.550
they do operation based
on a given element

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:19.160
and they produce some
number of output elements.

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:23.380
And to tie up with
the previous segment,

00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:25.190
Beam Python SDK
currently doesn't

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:27.680
have support for
state and timer APIs,

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:30.350
but that's one of the things
that's on our feature list

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:34.760
that we would like to add as
we advance to our roadmap.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:39.530
So with that, we conclude the
Python streaming-related part.

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:42.510
So stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:43.010
minute.

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:04.460
All right.

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:05.630
Thank you for the questions.

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:07.546
So I will read the
questions from the audience

00:38:07.546 --> 00:38:09.500
and start answering them.

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:12.260
The first question is, what
kind of windows do I have?

00:38:12.260 --> 00:38:18.650
Is it limited fixed,
sliding, and session windows?

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:21.360
In Beam, windowing
is just an API.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:25.040
It's possible to build any sort
of custom windowing functions

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:27.960
that satisfies your use case.

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:30.890
Beam, by default, comes with
these three type of windows--

00:38:30.890 --> 00:38:32.660
fixed, sliding, and session.

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:36.290
But there is no limit on how to
build or what kind of windows

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:36.800
to use.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.480
You can look at
some of our examples

00:38:38.480 --> 00:38:43.880
to find how to build
your own custom windows.

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:46.730
The next question is,
what are some tradeoffs

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:49.070
with various triggers?

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:52.560
Triggers at its core
allow you to customize

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:58.420
three things-- one, latency;
second, cost; and third,

00:38:58.420 --> 00:38:59.560
accuracy.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.090
Think of a use
case where you are

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:06.670
trying to show the top 10 users
for a mobile game leaderboard.

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:09.460
What you probably
need is low latency

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:12.120
so that your users
show new data quickly,

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:15.430
accuracy might not
be very important,

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:17.810
and you probably want
to reduce your costs.

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:20.060
And in a different case--
let's say this is a building

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:21.400
example--

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:24.490
that you want to get
amount of full billing

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:25.490
at the end of the month.

00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:28.030
In this case, probably latency
doesn't matter that much.

00:39:28.030 --> 00:39:30.480
If you get your
data an hour later,

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:32.260
the month ends will be OK.

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:33.790
But you really
want full precision

00:39:33.790 --> 00:39:36.250
in terms of how your
billing comes out.

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:38.650
And cost probably
doesn't matter that much.

00:39:38.650 --> 00:39:40.310
So triggers allow
you to customize

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:43.052
in these three dimensions.

00:39:43.052 --> 00:39:45.010
RYAN MCDOWELL: The next
question is, where do I

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.940
go to find more examples?

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:49.120
So the Apache Beam
SDK on GitHub actually

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:51.364
has an examples directory.

00:39:51.364 --> 00:39:52.780
And within this
directory, you can

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:55.690
find examples for simple
things like WordCount

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:59.230
all the way up to complex things
like running some processes

00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:01.340
within a ParDo function.

00:40:01.340 --> 00:40:03.840
We also provide
Dataflow templates.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.880
So Dataflow templates are
selectable on the Cloud Console

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:08.080
UI within a dropdown.

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:11.350
So say you're ingesting
data from Pub/Sub

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:13.540
and you just want to
output that to BigQuery.

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:15.160
You don't really have
that many transformations

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:16.576
that you want to
do, you just want

00:40:16.576 --> 00:40:18.130
to get that data
into your system.

00:40:18.130 --> 00:40:19.930
You can go to the
Dataflow Console UI

00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:21.760
and just select that
from a dropdown.

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:23.830
But we also publish
these templates

00:40:23.830 --> 00:40:27.100
on GitHub underneath the
Google Cloud Platform Dataflow

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:29.840
Templates repository.

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:33.040
The next question is, what are
some other common patterns?

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:34.840
Well, we actually
have a blog series

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:37.480
on common patterns
within Cloud Dataflow.

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:40.410
You can find these within the
Google Cloud Platform blog.

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:43.240
I believe they're called
"Use Case In Patterns--

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:47.537
Common Use Cases In Patterns
Within Cloud Dataflow."

00:40:47.537 --> 00:40:49.120
Just to mention some
of the other ones

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:51.220
that you may find
there, should be

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:56.890
like "Dealing With Bad
Data," "How Do I Make Calls

00:40:56.890 --> 00:41:01.750
To an External Database," "How
Do I Merge Windows Together,"

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:06.230
and "How Do I Handle Composite
Keys" for a group by key.

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:08.860
So check out those blogs to
find out more information

00:41:08.860 --> 00:41:11.650
about the common
patterns within Dataflow.

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:14.450
AHMED ALTAY: The next question
is, where is state stored?

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:17.860
And traditionally,
in Dataflow there's

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:22.730
an agent that handles
storing of state.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:24.560
And how it was
implemented, initially,

00:41:24.560 --> 00:41:27.720
was it used local disks
of the virtual machines.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.080
However, with the introduction
of streaming engines,

00:41:30.080 --> 00:41:34.250
some of these things are stored
in its own cloud service.

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:36.340
So it depends on what you use.

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:38.570
It's possible that your
local disk is used.

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:40.790
Or depending on if you are
using streaming engine,

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.370
it will be stored in
another cloud service.

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:48.130
RYAN MCDOWELL: The
next question is,

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:51.770
when do I use the state API
versus another hosted service?

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:56.230
So as you can imagine, storing
state in the pipeline itself,

00:41:56.230 --> 00:41:58.360
when do I use that
versus storing state

00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:01.330
in an external store
such as Cloud BigTable

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:05.140
or Redis, our memory store.

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:08.380
Well, essentially, storing
the state within your pipeline

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:11.650
allows you to have low
latency reads and writes

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:14.170
from that state.

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:15.790
And it allows you
to keep that state

00:42:15.790 --> 00:42:17.680
only for a particular
period of time, so

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:19.430
say, during your window.

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:21.280
So if you're using
another hosted service,

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:23.260
you may accumulate
that state over time

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:25.240
and have to do manual cleanup.

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:28.780
Another benefit of this
is that Dataflow actually

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:30.580
manages the state for you.

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:32.560
As you saw in the code
earlier, all I call

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:34.960
is read states or write states.

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:37.487
I don't have to worry about
setting up any infrastructure.

00:42:37.487 --> 00:42:39.820
I don't have to worry about
managing that infrastructure

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:42.580
or monitoring it because it's
all handled on the backend

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:46.214
by Google Cloud's
Dataflow service.

00:42:46.214 --> 00:42:48.130
AHMED ALTAY: And the
last question on the list

00:42:48.130 --> 00:42:52.600
is, what are the limitations of
state API in terms of latency,

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:56.290
size, or anything else?

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:59.230
As we just talk in one of
our previous questions, state

00:42:59.230 --> 00:43:01.060
itself is stored either
on a local disk or

00:43:01.060 --> 00:43:02.840
and that different service.

00:43:02.840 --> 00:43:06.130
So I think a lot of
size to your state

00:43:06.130 --> 00:43:08.740
will mean that a
considerable amount of data

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:11.480
is moving just because
it's part of the state.

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:13.210
So if you're in
one of these cases,

00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:15.817
you could consider
re-architecting

00:43:15.817 --> 00:43:17.650
your [INAUDIBLE] such
that some of this data

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:21.580
is actually part of
your main input data.

00:43:21.580 --> 00:43:26.870
In terms of latency,
again, the state

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:28.760
needs to be shuffled
at [INAUDIBLE]..

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:31.310
So there will be some
amount of latency,

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:33.921
but it will not be a
considerable amount.

00:43:33.921 --> 00:43:36.170
And the final thing that you
should be considering is,

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:37.836
as we mentioned during
our presentation,

00:43:37.836 --> 00:43:41.720
is that the state data itself
needs to be serializable.

00:43:41.720 --> 00:43:45.110
So as long as you pick a
type that is serializable,

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:48.990
you can use any type of
[INAUDIBLE] for your state.

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:52.910
So with that, we
[INAUDIBLE] the questions.

00:43:52.910 --> 00:43:54.920
RYAN MCDOWELL: Stay tuned
for the next section--

